  <div class="section-label">Community Overview</div>
  <h2>Hooper's Role in Weber County's Agricultural Heritage</h2>

  <p>Hooper is an incorporated city in southern Weber County, approximately 12 miles southwest of Ogden.{% cite "hooper-city-official" %} Historically, the area has been defined by agricultural operations, irrigation infrastructure, and open space. The community sits between Ogden to the east and the agricultural flatlands extending west toward the Great Salt Lake.</p>

  <p>SR-177 Phase 3 (the Weber County extension) runs along 5100 West, which is the boundary between Hooper and West Haven.{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol1-nov2026" %} UDOT's Draft State Environmental Study, published November 6, 2026, places the freeway west of 5100 West south of the 5500 South interchange and east of 5100 West north of it.{% cite "udot-draft-ses-appendix-nov2026" %} It crosses agricultural parcels and canals and requires coordination with irrigation districts and water infrastructure that has served the community for over a century.</p>

  <div class="callout info">
    <p><strong>Draft design elements near Hooper</strong> (subject to change before the Final State Environmental Study):{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol1-nov2026" %}</p>
    <ul style="margin: 8px 0 0 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
      <li>A diamond interchange at 5500 South (SR-97), with a grade-separated pedestrian crossing on the west side</li>
      <li>5300 West extended from 5100 South to 5900 South as a new two-lane frontage road, and 5100 West kept as a frontage road between about 6000 South and 5500 South</li>
      <li>5900 South and 5725 South removed between about 5150 West and 5100 West, and the 5300 South / 5100 West intersection replaced by a cul-de-sac west of the canal</li>
      <li>Underpasses keeping 5100 South open and carrying the planned Hooper Slough Trail under the freeway</li>
      <li>The Hooper Canal and Hooper Branch Canal would be piped where the freeway crosses them, which the draft finds is not an adverse effect on these historic canals</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="section-label">Agricultural Context</div>
  <h2>Land Use and Agricultural Operations</h2>

  <p>The Hooper area has historically supported a mix of agricultural operations, including:</p>

  <ul style="margin: 12px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
    <li><strong>Field crops</strong> (alfalfa, small grains, sugar beets)</li>
    <li><strong>Livestock operations</strong> (dairy, cattle ranching)</li>
    <li><strong>Orchards and nurseries</strong> (in eastern foothill areas)</li>
    <li><strong>Hay and forage production</strong> (supporting regional livestock)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Agricultural land in the Hooper area remains economically viable but faces ongoing pressure from regional development, water scarcity, and conversion to residential uses. Many parcels operate under the Farmland Assessment property tax program, which requires agricultural use to qualify for reduced tax rates.</p>

  <p><strong>For property owners:</strong> If your parcel is assessed as farmland, SR-177 acquisition or severance may affect your eligibility for farmland assessment. You may want to consult a property tax professional about the implications before negotiations with UDOT.</p>

  <div class="section-label">Irrigation Infrastructure</div>
  <h2>Water Districts and Irrigation Rights</h2>

  <p>The Hooper area is served by multiple irrigation entities that have managed water delivery for over 100 years:</p>

  <ul style="margin: 12px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
    <li><strong>Hooper Water Improvement District (HWID)</strong> — Primary provider of irrigation water via the Malad-Shepherd Canal</li>
    <li><strong>Davis & Weber Counties Canal Company (D&WC Canal)</strong> — Large regional canal providing water from the Weber River</li>
    <li><strong>Hooper Irrigation Company</strong> — Local water cooperative managing secondary delivery</li>
    <li><strong>Private wells</strong> — Some agricultural parcels supplement with groundwater</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Why this matters for SR-177:</strong></p>
  <ul style="margin: 12px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
    <li>Major irrigation canals must be crossed or relocated if they intersect the SR-177 corridor</li>
    <li>Groundwater impacts from construction or grade changes could affect well yield</li>
    <li>Alignment changes could block irrigation delivery access to downstream properties</li>
    <li>Water rights held by property owners (appropriation shares in canal companies) retain value even if land is acquired</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>For property owners:</strong> If you have water rights or rely on irrigation for your operation, document your shares/allocations now. Compensation and mitigation can be more complex when land, water delivery, and remaining agricultural operations are connected. Consult with the irrigation district and a water law attorney if your parcel is near the corridor.</p>

  <div class="section-label">Hooper Water Improvement District</div>
  <h2>HWID: Service Area, Capacity & Project Coordination</h2>

  <p>The Hooper Water Improvement District is a local water entity serving the Hooper area. Because the SR-177 study corridor crosses agricultural land and irrigation infrastructure, future design work may require coordination with HWID, canal companies, and affected landowners.</p>

  <p><strong>Service Area:</strong> Property owners should verify their own water provider, shares, and delivery infrastructure directly with HWID, canal companies, or title/water-rights records. Irrigation service areas and ownership arrangements can vary parcel by parcel.</p>

  <p><strong>Potential SR-177 coordination issues:</strong></p>
  <ul style="margin: 12px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
    <li>Canal relocations or crossings may be required if SR-177 Phase 3 intersects canal infrastructure</li>
    <li>Drainage and groundwater management during construction must be coordinated to avoid damage to irrigation systems</li>
    <li>Post-construction, the district must ensure water delivery access is maintained</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>For property owners:</strong> Track Hooper City and water-provider meetings to stay informed on any coordination with UDOT. A water provider's position on impacts and mitigation may affect property valuation, severance, and access discussions.</p>

  <div class="section-label">Opposition & Community Concerns</div>
  <h2>Local Responses to SR-177 Phase 3</h2>

  <p>Some Hooper-area residents and agricultural interests have expressed concerns about the Phase 3 extension. Key themes from community input include:</p>

  <ul style="margin: 12px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
    <li><strong>Agricultural land loss:</strong> Concern that the highway will fragment agricultural operations and accelerate conversion to non-agricultural uses</li>
    <li><strong>Water infrastructure disruption:</strong> Worry that construction and alignment could damage or require relocation of irrigation canals on which the community depends</li>
    <li><strong>Environmental impact:</strong> Concerns about wetlands, groundwater, and habitat in the sensitive lakebed environment</li>
    <li><strong>Compensation adequacy:</strong> Questions about whether UDOT compensation for acquired land and severance will be fair to affected farmers</li>
    <li><strong>Property fragmentation:</strong> Worry that remaining land (after SR-177 takes a portion) will be economically unviable for farming</li>
    <li><strong>Construction impacts:</strong> Concerns about dust, noise, traffic disruption during 3–5 year construction period</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Some opposition groups have also questioned whether SR-177 is necessary for regional mobility and have advocated for alternatives (e.g., improvements to I-15, US-89, or local roads) instead of a new limited-access highway.</p>

  <p><strong>UDOT's position:</strong> The Draft State Environmental Study, published November 6, 2026, finds that the extension meets the project's purpose and need, and that leaving it unbuilt would not. Its analysis covers traffic, relocations, noise, farmland, water rights, wetlands, and historic resources.{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol1-nov2026" %} UDOT met with the Hooper Water Improvement District, Hooper Irrigation Company, and canal companies during the study, and says wells or diversions affected by the project would be relocated or the owner compensated for the water right.{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol1-nov2026" %} UDOT is taking comments through December 11, 2026, and will respond to them in the Final State Environmental Study.{% cite "udot-ses-schedule" %}</p>

  <div class="section-label">What Property Owners Should Do Now</div>
  <h2>Preparation for Phase 3 Planning & State Environmental Study</h2>

  <p>If you own property in or near the Hooper area (generally south of 4000 South, between 5000–5500 West):</p>

  <ol style="margin: 12px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 2;">
    <li><strong>Locate your property in the study area.</strong> Visit the SR-177 project page at <a href="https://udotinput.utah.gov/sr177extension" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">udotinput.utah.gov/sr177extension</a>, register for updates, and review published study materials as they are released.</li>
    <li><strong>Document water rights and irrigation shares.</strong> If you use irrigation or have well water, verify your water shares with HWID and any canal companies. Keep records of water rights documents.</li>
    <li><strong>Assess your options now that the draft maps are out.</strong> Check your parcel in the Draft State Environmental Study's Volume 2 mapbooks, then decide whether you may want to sell early to UDOT or explore severance/mitigation options. A local real estate appraiser familiar with agricultural property can help you understand your land's value under different scenarios.</li>
    <li><strong>Attend HWID meetings.</strong> Stay informed on the district's negotiations with UDOT regarding canal relocations and mitigation.</li>
    <li><strong>Request project updates.</strong> Register through the SR-177 study page at <a href="https://udotinput.utah.gov/sr177extension" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">udotinput.utah.gov/sr177extension</a> so you receive notice of hearing materials, comment deadlines, and the Final State Environmental Study.</li>
    <li><strong>Consult professionals.</strong> Consider engaging a real estate attorney and a property tax advisor who can help you understand acquisition, water rights, and tax implications.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="section-label">Timeline & Next Steps</div>
  <h2>From Now Through Final Design</h2>

  <p><strong>November 6 – December 11, 2026:</strong> UDOT published the Draft State Environmental Study, which identifies a Preferred Alternative, and is taking formal comments. The online presentation was held November 9, and the in-person public hearing was set for November 10 at Roy Junior High School in Roy. Invitation-only neighborhood meetings for affected owners and tenants were held in early November.{% cite "udot-ses-schedule" %}{% cite "udot-ses-public-hearing-nov2026" %}</p>
  <p><strong>Early 2027:</strong> UDOT responds to comments, then publishes the Final State Environmental Study for approval.{% cite "udot-ses-schedule" %}</p>
  <p><strong>2026–2028:</strong> Phase 2 Davis County extension under construction (precedent for Phase 3 timeline and impacts).{% cite "wfrc-transcom-nov2025-udot-sr177-study" %}</p>
  <p><strong>After the study:</strong> Phase 3 depends on funding. The draft study says it could be built in two phases as funding becomes available, with the 1800 North to 5500 South section first and 5500 South to 4000 South later. The regional plan assumes construction funding in 2033–2042, but UDOT's draft 2027–2032 program includes none.{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol1-nov2026" %}{% cite "udot-draft-stip-2027-2032" %}</p>

  <p><strong>Key action dates:</strong></p>
  <ul style="margin: 12px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
    <li>Now – December 11, 2026 — Draft State Environmental Study public review; check the Volume 2 maps and submit comments by December 11 if you have concerns{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol2-nov2026" %}</li>
    <li>November 10, 2026 — In-person public hearing, 4:30–7:30 p.m., Roy Junior High School</li>
    <li>Early 2027 — Final State Environmental Study expected</li>
    <li>2026–2029 — Phase 2 construction underway in Davis County (watch for impacts and precedent){% cite "udot-2026-construction" %}</li>
    <li>Post-State Environmental Study — Phase 3 acquisition timing depends on formal funding and design decisions</li>
  </ul>

